NAME
    Data::Shared - Path-addressed facade over the Data-*-Shared family

SYNOPSIS
        use Data::Shared qw(shared_get shared_set shared_exists shared_call
                            shared_push shared_pop shared_compile sharedc_get);

        my $root = Data::Shared->new;
        $root->mount('cache', 'hashmap:ii', undef, 100_000);
        $root->mount('visitors', 'hyperloglog', undef, 14);
        $root->mount('jobs', 'queue:int', undef, 1024);

        shared_set($root, '/cache/42', 100);
        shared_get($root, '/cache/42');            # 100
        shared_exists($root, '/cache/42');         # 1

        shared_call($root, '/visitors', 'add', 'alice');
        shared_call($root, '/visitors', 'count');  # ~1

        shared_push($root, '/jobs', 42);
        shared_pop($root, '/jobs');                # 42

        my $app = Data::Shared->new('/tmp/demo.shared');
        $app->mount('cache', 'hashmap:ii', undef, 100_000)
            unless $app->mount_info('cache');      # a mount is one-time topology
        shared_set($app, '/cache/42', 100);
        my $same = Data::Shared->open('/tmp/demo.shared');
        shared_get($same, '/cache/42');            # 100, from another handle

        my $cp = shared_compile('/cache/42');
        sharedc_get($root, $cp);

        use Data::Shared qw(shareda_get shareda_set);
        $root->mount('names', 'hashmap:ss', undef, 100_000);
        shareda_set($root, ['names', 'a/b'], 1);   # a component may contain '/'
        shareda_get($root, ['names', 'a/b']);

DESCRIPTION
    Data::Shared wraps all 39 "Data-*-Shared" distributions behind one
    path-based API inspired by Data::Path::XS. A root object holds named
    mounts; a slash path resolves to a mount plus an intra-structure
    operation:

        /cache/42        mount "cache", key 42
        /lat/0/5         mount "lat", indices 0, 5 (NDArray)
        /visitors        mount "visitors" (used with shared_call)

    A path resolves in two levels, not by walking: there is nothing to walk
    inside an opaque shared-memory handle, so the components select a mount
    and the rest is the adapter's business. Composite mounts ("DEEP PATHS
    (COMPOSITES)") add one child address per level. The three tiers (string,
    array, compiled) otherwise mirror Data::Path::XS, except that its
    keyword syntax is not provided: requesting ":keywords" croaks.
    "PERFORMANCE" says why dispatch stays in Perl, and
    Data::Shared::Cookbook has a recipe per leaf.

    Linux only, 64-bit Perl, matching every leaf. Handles are never cloned
    into ithreads ("CLONE_SKIP").

    The leaf dists are recommended rather than required, so installing this
    facade does not compile all 39 of them. Install the ones you mount;
    mounting a type whose dist is absent croaks naming the dist to install.
    "cpanm --with-recommends Data::Shared" installs every leaf. One
    dependency is not implied by what you mount: a composite on an anonymous
    root locks its children with Data-Sync-Shared, so install that dist too.
    File-backed composites use the directory lock and do not need it.

ROOT AND MOUNTS
  Data::Shared->new([$dir])
    Without a directory, creates an anonymous root. With a directory,
    creates it if needed; if a manifest.json already exists there it is
    replayed (every mount reopened), otherwise an empty manifest is written.
    When the root is file-backed and a mount's constructor arguments are
    empty, start with "undef", or start with ':file', the facade supplies
    "$dir/<name>.shm" as the backing file.

  Data::Shared->open($dir)
    Requires an existing directory and manifest. The complete manifest
    schema, mount specs, and mount backing paths are checked before
    constructing handles. Missing storage is an error; opening never
    recreates an empty backing file. Composite descendant files are checked
    when the child is opened lazily. Native backends validate their own file
    headers.

  Data::Shared->open_sealed($dir)
    Opens a store whose leaves have been frozen, attaching each sealed leaf
    read-only so the replay succeeds. "open" cannot do this -- a frozen file
    refuses the writable constructor -- and "open_readonly" gives a root
    whose lifecycle verbs croak, so without this a store had no reachable
    teardown once the process that froze it exited. Reads work; data writes
    are refused by the leaves themselves; the reason to open a store this
    way is "unlink_all".

  Data::Shared->open_readonly($dir, strict => 1)
    Replays the manifest, opening each leaf with "new_readonly" where the
    leaf supports it and with the normal constructor where it does not. A
    leaf with a native read-only open (21 of the 39 families, the "freeze"
    entries in Data::Shared::Registry; "hashmap" and "bloomfilter" among
    them) requires a frozen file: on a live one its "new_readonly" croaks,
    which aborts the whole replay with the leaf's message nested under
    "failed to replay manifest". Freeze the store in the producing process
    first. The generic mutating verbs ("set", "delete", "incr", "push",
    "pop"), every root lifecycle write ("mount", "unmount", "freeze_all",
    "unlink_all") and native "unlink", "destroy" and "freeze" croak on any
    read-only root. Other native methods, reached through "shared_call" or
    "handle", keep whatever permissions that leaf's own file gives them: the
    root flag by itself does not make them read-only.

    "strict => 1" closes that gap by refusing the store instead. Every
    backend in the declared tree, lazy composite leaves included, must
    provide "new_readonly" or be immutable (PerfectHash), and is rejected
    before any handle is opened, so the producer has to freeze whatever
    needs a frozen file first. Raw handles still expose their backend's own
    API, and the filesystem still decides what a process may write directly.

  $root->mount($name, $spec, @ctor_args)
    Mounts a structure under $name (which may itself contain "/", for nested
    namespaces; longest-prefix wins). $spec may be:

    *   a short spec: 'hashmap', 'hashmap:ii', 'buffer:i64', 'queue:int',
        'sync:semaphore', 'sortedset:strings', etc. Short specs are
        all-lowercase (mixed case is accepted and resolves to the lowercase
        canonical form). See Data::Shared::Registry for the full inventory.

    *   a full class name: 'Data::HashMap::Shared::SS'.

    *   a hashref: "{ spec => 'hashmap:ss', args => [...] }" or with an
        explicit "ctor" (for "new_weighted"/"new_decayed" style
        constructors, also available as 'reservoir:weighted' /
        'topk:decayed').

    *   an already-constructed handle (transient; not written to the
        manifest).

    *   a coderef factory called as "$factory->($root, $name)" (transient).

    *   a composite spec (arrayref or hashref with "child"): see "DEEP PATHS
        (COMPOSITES)".

    Returns the native handle (for a composite mount, a Data::Shared::Node
    for the mount). "$root->handle($path)" also returns it, and any native
    method can always be reached that way or via "shared_call".

    Configuration arrays and hashes are copied before construction. Cycles
    are rejected; constructor arguments that cannot be serialized make the
    mount transient. Failed mount publication restores the previous
    namespace and removes backing paths created by that attempt. Existing
    files are retained. Factories manage their own external side effects.
    Nested mount mutations inside a factory are rejected.

    Every argument has to survive the manifest, because replaying it is how
    each later process reopens the store. "Inf", "NaN" and a lone surrogate
    are refused at mount time rather than published as text nothing can
    parse back. Strings are recorded as bytes, and a string held as
    characters is encoded to its UTF-8 bytes first, uniformly from U+0080
    up: the character "\x{e9}" and the single byte "\xe9" are therefore two
    different paths naming two different files.

    Special cases: "perfecthash" is an immutable build-then-load structure,
    so a 'perfecthash' spec takes either a built file path or a "{ build_int
    => \@keys, path => $file }" / "build_str" hashref. A build spec creates
    a missing file once; an existing file is loaded without rebuilding. To
    supply one some other way, pass the factory or the loaded handle as the
    spec, as for any other type. "sortedset:strings" takes "mount('names',
    'sortedset:strings', { max => 100_000 })"; "set"/"keys" backing paths
    are auto-filled for file-backed roots. A backing path you name yourself
    must be absolute, because it is published in the manifest and resolved
    again by every process that opens the store; a relative one would
    resolve against each process's own working directory. Paths the facade
    derives from the root directory are unaffected, and are relative exactly
    when that directory is.

    Backing paths already owned by another mount are rejected before
    construction, including aliases produced by filename sanitization (e.g.
    "a/b" and "a__b") and string SortedSet sidecars. Mount the same native
    handle under another name to share it intentionally; an "unmount" that
    would unlink storage another mount still names is refused, so retire the
    aliases first.

  $root->unmount($name), $root->mounts, $root->mount_info($name)
    Lifecycle and introspection for mounts. Names are canonicalized as
    "mount" canonicalizes them, so "/a/" and "a" name one mount.
    "mount_names" is a synonym for "mounts"; "available_types" (class or
    root method) lists the 39 short spec names and "registry" returns the
    Data::Shared::Registry table itself.

    "unmount" keeps the mount's storage and records it in the manifest, so a
    later mount of the same name adopts it. To reclaim it while the store
    stays in service, mount the name again and "unmount" it with "unlink =>
    1"; "unlink_all" reclaims it too, from any process that opens the root,
    but takes the whole store with it. That record costs one manifest entry
    per retained store until something reclaims it, and every topology
    change republishes the manifest, so a program that rotates mounts under
    fresh names should pass "unlink => 1" rather than accumulate them.
    "unmount($name, unlink => 1)" removes the storage instead, under the
    same directory lock that published its removal, and a native "unlink" or
    "destroy" reached through "shared_call" at a mount does the same -- a
    manifest naming storage that is gone makes the store refuse to open at
    all. See "SHARING A ROOT" for what another process sees while this
    happens.

  $root->dir, $root->readonly, $root->manifest_path
    Accessors: the root's directory, whether it was opened read-only, and
    the path of its manifest. The directory and the manifest path are both
    "undef" for an anonymous root.

  $root->sync_all, $root->freeze_all, $root->unlink_all, $root->stats, $root->frozen
    Proxies applied to every mount whose handle supports the method, in
    mount order. "stats" returns a hashref per mount: the leaf's own stats
    where it has them, and "{ class => ref $handle }" where it does not. A
    composite mount counts as its whole subtree: parent, child index and
    every registered child, open or not. "freeze_all" returns the number of
    handles it sealed, so 0 is a success: either everything was already
    sealed, or no mount can be. "frozen" tells those apart -- but only 21 of
    the 39 families can be sealed at all. "perfecthash" is immutable and
    needs no sealing, so it does not block the predicate; a store holding
    any of the other 17 ("buffer", "queue", "stack", "deque", "heap", "log",
    "pool", "pubsub", "reqrep", "ringbuffer", "sync", "timingwheel",
    "hiertimingwheel", "reservoir", "topk", "segmenttree", "disjointset") or
    the "sortedset:strings" variant can never report "frozen". "frozen" is a
    predicate and does not say which mount blocks it; "open_readonly($dir,
    strict => 1)" does, by naming the first unsupported mount and class it
    meets. Freezing is also a one-way door for the store: afterwards "open"
    refuses it for every process, including the one that froze it, and
    "open_sealed" is how it is reopened. A mounted native handle is sealed
    with the rest, so the caller's own object becomes read-only.
    "unlink_all" removes the mounts, the manifest, and every retained store
    the manifest names -- whichever process unmounted it, since the ledger
    is shared -- but leaves .manifest.lock, which another process may still
    be holding, so the directory is not empty afterwards. "freeze_all" and
    "unlink_all" both croak on a read-only root.

SHARING A ROOT
    Topology changes take a directory lock and bump a revision, so they
    serialise between processes. A root that was opened before another
    writer's change gets a "stale manifest revision" error from "mount",
    "unmount", "freeze_all" and "unlink_all", raised before any constructor
    or factory runs: reopen the root and retry against the current
    configuration. A store destroyed and rebuilt at the same directory is
    refused the same way, as "this store was replaced", since an identical
    rebuild reproduces both the revision and the published bytes. Ordinary
    reads and writes never check the revision, so they neither fail nor
    refresh; a write to a mount another process retired with "unlink => 1"
    is one of those, and succeeds into a mapping whose file is gone, where
    nothing will read it back.

    A root keeps its own mount namespace until it is reopened, and a
    composite child it already holds open is part of that namespace. If
    another process destroys and recreates the store, this root goes on
    reading the child it holds, and "exists" and "children" go on reporting
    it, until something makes it take the composite lock again and the
    instance check fires. It never crosses into the new store's storage --
    it is answering for the old one -- so a worker that must notice a
    teardown should reopen rather than wait to be told.

    Configure topology from one coordinator and let the workers open the
    result; that is the pattern the revision protocol is built for.

PATH FORMAT
    Components are separated by "/". A leading "/" is optional, repeated and
    trailing slashes are tolerated, and the empty path addresses the root
    ("shared_get" returns the root, "shared_exists" is true, mutating the
    root croaks) -- all mirroring Data::Path::XS. The first component(s)
    select the mount (longest prefix match); the remainder is interpreted by
    the mount's adapter:

    *   Map-like (HashMap, Buffer, RadixTree, Intern, Pool, PerfectHash):
        one key/index component. RadixTree instead joins the remainder with
        "/" so route-style keys work in string paths.

    *   Set-like (Bloom family, BitSet, RoaringBitmap, SortedSet,
        DisjointSet): one member component.

    *   Indexed (NDArray, Fenwick, Fenwick2D, SegmentTree, Graph): all
        remaining components are indices/coordinates.

    *   Queue-, sketch- and sync-like: no remainder; use
        "shared_push"/"shared_pop"/"shared_call", which take the value as an
        argument. The keyed sketches "countminsketch" and "topk" are the
        exception: they require exactly one component, the item, and croak
        without it. "ringbuffer"'s "get" may read one, a sequence number.

    Map-, set- and queue-like mounts croak on a longer path rather than
    using the first component and discarding the rest. RadixTree, which
    joins, is unaffected.

    Mount selection takes the longest name that matches the joined
    components, so a nested mount wins over the same string reached through
    a shorter one: with "users" and "users/sales" mounted, "/users/sales/eu"
    addresses the "users/sales" mount, not the "sales" record or composite
    child of "users". Array paths join the same way, a component containing
    "/" included, so do not give a nested mount a name that spells out an
    address its parent also serves.

    No escaping is provided in the string tier: keys containing "/" or the
    empty string need the array tier.

GENERIC VERBS
    "shared_get", "shared_set", "shared_delete", "shared_exists" and
    "shared_incr" are honest sugar over native methods, defined per type in
    Data::Shared::Registry (e.g. HashMap "put/get/remove/exists", RadixTree
    "insert/lookup/delete/exists", SortedSet "add/score/remove", Intern
    "intern/id_of"). Where a verb would lie (a plain Bloom filter has no
    delete; unkeyed sketches and sync primitives have no keyed get/set) it
    croaks with guidance to use "shared_call" instead of faking it.

    Each generic verb is also a method on the root, taking the function's
    arguments without the leading $root: "$root->get('/cache/42')", "set",
    "delete", "exists", "incr", "push", "pop" and "$root->call($path,
    $method, @args)". "$root->compile($path)" is "shared_compile".

    Where it is defined, "shared_exists" always returns 1 or 0, an index
    outside the structure included; where a type has no honest "exists"
    ("ndarray", "fenwick2d") it croaks like any other absent verb.
    "shared_incr" uses "incr_by" when a delta is given and the leaf supports
    it; a leaf with neither "incr_by" nor a delta form of "incr" (most
    "buffer" variants) rejects the delta from its own XS, so use
    "shared_call" there.

    These are the leaf's own behaviours showing through, and worth knowing
    before you rely on them:

    *   "shared_set($path, 0)" clears for BitSet, but counts as an add for
        RoaringBitmap and the Cuckoo and counting Bloom filters, whose value
        argument is ignored. Use "shared_delete" to remove.

    *   "shared_delete" on a "buffer" writes the variant's zero, which for
        "buffer:str" is the one-character string "0".

    *   A write can fail by returning false instead of croaking:
        "sortedset:strings" and "intern" derive a string arena of 32 bytes
        per declared entry, so longer members exhaust it early and
        "shared_set" returns "undef". Pass a larger "arena", or a larger
        third constructor argument for "intern", and check the return value.
        A full "queue", "deque", "stack" or "heap" drops a "shared_push" the
        same way, as does a "shared_set" past the end of a "buffer".

    *   Keys cross into XS as bytes, so encode wide characters first. A
        character above U+00FF is accepted by the HashMap family and the
        integer SortedSet, and fatal in RadixTree, Intern, the filter
        family, CountMinSketch and "sortedset:strings" (whose every verb
        dies).

    *   An integer-addressed leaf numifies a non-integer key, so 'abc'
        addresses element 0. The "key_int" families ("buffer",
        "disjointset", "fenwick", "fenwick2d", "graph", "ndarray",
        "segmenttree") refuse it, on every verb but "exists"; "bitset",
        "roaringbitmap", "ringbuffer"'s "get" and the integer "hashmap"
        variants address element 0, with a warning under "-w"; "pool"
        croaks, its slot 0 not being allocated.

  shared_call($root, $path, $method, @args)
    The escape hatch that guarantees full coverage: resolves $path to a
    mount, prepends any remaining path components to @args, and calls the
    native $method. Every native operation of every leaf is reachable this
    way, including those with no generic verb. On a composite mount the
    first remaining component instead selects the child, and the rest are
    passed to the child's method.

    "unlink" and "destroy" are the exception: they retire storage, so they
    are only accepted where a registration can be retired with it, at a
    mount or at a composite child, and are refused at a record below one. At
    a composite child they remove the child's registration too, the same as
    "shared_delete" on that address.

        shared_call($root, '/visitors', 'add_many', ['a', 'b']);
        shared_call($root, '/fenwick', 'prefix', 500);
        shared_call($root, '/sem', 'acquire', 1.5);

  shared_push($root, $path, @values) / shared_pop($root, $path, @args)
    Queue sugar mapping to the native pair per type (Queue "push/pop", Deque
    "push_back/pop_front", Stack "push/pop", Heap "push/pop" with
    priority+value, Log "append" (no pop), PubSub "publish" (no pop),
    RingBuffer "write" (no pop)). Unsupported directions croak.

ARRAY PATH API
    "shareda_get/set/delete/exists/incr/call/push/pop" take an arrayref of
    components instead of a slash string. Binary-safe; components may
    contain "/" or be empty strings, subject to the resolution rule in "PATH
    FORMAT".

COMPILED PATH API
    shared_compile($path) (string or arrayref) returns a
    Data::Shared::Compiled object holding parsed components; "sharedc_*"
    functions reuse it without re-parsing, mirroring
    "path_compile"/"pathc_*" in Data::Path::XS. On first use against a root
    the compiled path also caches the resolved mount, verb and key, so later
    calls go straight to the native method; the cache is invalidated
    automatically when mounts change. Compiled objects are still
    per-process: recompile after "fork" if the child mounts differently.

DEEP PATHS (COMPOSITES)
    A composite mount nests structures: the parent gives each child a stable
    address, and the facade keeps one child instance per address (no leaf
    can physically contain another). Specs are nested arrayrefs (constructor
    arguments exclude the backing path, which the facade supplies per child;
    a leading "undef" placeholder is tolerated) or hashrefs with
    "spec"/"args"/"child". In the arrayref form a trailing arrayref is
    always the child spec, so a leaf whose constructor takes a trailing
    arrayref argument (such as SpatialHash grid wrapping) must use the
    hashref form:

        $root->mount('users', ['hashmap:ss', 100_000, ['bloomfilter', 10_000, 0.01]]);
        $root->mount('leaders', ['sortedset:strings', { max => 100_000 }, ['bloomfilter', 10_000, 0.01]]);
        $root->mount('atlas', ['hashmap:ss', 100_000, ['kdtree', 2, 100_000, ['hashmap:ss', 10_000]]]);

        shared_set($root, '/users/alice/event-42', 1);
        shared_exists($root, '/users/alice/event-42');
        shared_set($root, '/leaders/alice', 1500);           # SortedSet score
        shared_get($root, '/atlas/europe/42/name');

    Parents must be addressable: "hashmap" and "sortedset" (child per
    key/member; for SortedSet the path ending at the child still reads and
    writes its score), plus "kdtree" and "graph" as id-parents (child per
    point/node id; geometry is added through
    "$root->node('/atlas/europe')->call('add', [$x, $y], $id)", and a deep
    write for an id does not require the point to exist). Bloom filters,
    sketches, queues, stacks, logs, sync and timing mounts cannot be parents
    (probabilistic or transient: their members cannot be enumerated or
    stably addressed) and are rejected at mount time; use them as child
    leaves.

    Semantics at an intermediate path: "shared_get" returns a
    Data::Shared::Node (for SortedSet, the score instead), "shared_exists"
    reports whether a child is registered, "shared_delete" deletes the whole
    subtree, and "shared_set" croaks except for the SortedSet score case.
    "$root->node($path)" (also exported as "shared_node") returns a cursor
    with relative-path
    "get/set/delete/exists/incr/push/pop/call/call_at/node/children/handle"
    methods; "$root->children($path)" ("shared_children") lists registered
    child addresses. "handle" on a child leaf path returns its native
    handle. Reads of a missing child never create one; writes create it
    lazily, and so does any "call" there, since the method may mutate. Probe
    with "shared_exists", not a read-only call, or a loop over candidate
    addresses will register every one of them and exhaust the child budget.

    Each composite declares "max_children" (default 10_000) and "cache_size"
    open children (default 32, LRU close/reopen) via the hashref spec form
    or a trailing "{max_children => ..., cache_size => ...}" in the arrayref
    form. One child instance is one backing segment, so high-cardinality
    parents need an explicit budget. "cache_size" bounds one composite's own
    cache; a nested composite is itself a cached entry with its own cache
    plus its parent and index segments, so a deep tree's open-segment
    ceiling multiplies per level. The budget sizes the child index when the
    composite is created and cannot be raised afterwards: mounting a name
    over storage an "unmount" retained with a larger "max_children" is
    refused, since the index file cannot grow. "unmount($name, unlink => 1)"
    first, and build the subtree again. Every child is also an entry in the
    parent leaf, so whichever of the two is smaller is the real ceiling:
    size the parent for the budget, or a "sortedset" parent of four fills
    after four children whatever "max_children" says. Both ceilings assume
    short addresses: a 256-byte address exhausts the child index near half
    of "max_children", and a string-keyed parent's own arena can bind first.
    Anonymous composites retain all opened children until subtree deletion
    or root destruction, because an anonymous segment cannot be reopened
    after eviction; "cache_size" bounds only file-backed child caches. Use a
    file-backed root when processes need to discover children created after
    "fork". File-backed roots store children under .children/ next to the
    manifest and reopen them on replay; composite mounts support "sync_all",
    "stats" (with child counts), "freeze_all", and subtree deletion.
    Compiled paths over composites stay correct across child creation,
    eviction, and deletion because they re-dispatch to the child per call.

    Allocation, construction, publication, score-only registration, and
    subtree deletion use the composite lifecycle lock. Failed construction
    rolls back new registrations, counts, and newly created paths; existing
    scores and reservations are preserved. Allocated child IDs are never
    reused. Cached leaf operations use the backend's own locking. An
    operation already in progress during deletion may complete against the
    old instance; an explicit native handle continues to refer to that
    instance after recreation.

    The shared child counter is maintained under the lifecycle lock, and is
    rebuilt only when an unfinished mutation marker is found: on opening a
    writable composite, or when a lifecycle lock encounters one. A holder
    that dies mid-section releases the lock either way: the kernel releases
    a file-backed composite's file lock, and an anonymous composite's lock
    is recovered from the dead owner, at the cost of about 20 kB of shared
    mapping per anonymous composite instance. Unpublished directories may
    remain and are retained for explicit recovery: each holds its child's
    storage, no API reports them, and only "unlink_all" reclaims them, so a
    long-running producer that is killed repeatedly accumulates them until
    the store is torn down. A preexisting unpublished directory for a
    reserved child is reported as an error. This provides exception rollback
    and limited interruption recovery, without promising power-loss
    transactions or automatic orphan cleanup.

PERFORMANCE
    The facade is pure Perl; every operation ends in the leaf's XS, so
    per-call overhead depends on the tier. A sample run of bench/overhead.pl
    on one x86_64 host, reading one integer key, rates taken from measured
    CPU time:

        native handle    0.08 us/op
        compiled         0.65 us/op
        array path       2.60 us/op
        shared_call      2.60 us/op
        string path      3.40 us/op

    Those are one host's figures and the string tier varied by about 15% run
    to run; the ordering is the durable part, not the values.

    The string and array tiers pay for path parsing and mount resolution on
    every call. A compiled path skips both: a live binding is checked only
    for root identity and generation, and the remaining arguments reach the
    bound closure without being copied again. Use compiled paths, or the
    native handle from "handle", in hot loops. The handle is the floor and
    costs no facade call at all, which is why dispatch is not in XS: an XS
    dispatcher would speed up a middle tier that callers can bypass. Run
    bench/benchmark.pl, bench/overhead.pl and bench/mounts.pl on your
    hardware; every figure quoted here comes from one of them.
    bench/composite.pl measures deep reads with a warm child cache and with
    repeated cache eviction. Composite compiled paths still resolve the
    child registry on every call; their cost differs from flat compiled
    paths.

    Creating flat mounts costs quadratic total time in their number: each
    one validates and republishes the whole manifest, so the marginal cost
    of one more doubles as the count doubles. bench/mounts.pl measures this;
    on the same host the average was 1.4 ms per mount at 25 mounts and 8.0
    ms at 200, i.e. 1.6 s to create 200, with the 200th alone costing 16 ms.
    Keys are not affected: writing 10_000 keys into one flat mount took 38
    ms and never touches the manifest, which that script also asserts.
    Opening such a root is superlinear too, and paid by every process that
    opens it: 0.15 s to replay 400 mounts on the same host. A namespace with
    hundreds of mounts belongs in a composite mount ("DEEP PATHS
    (COMPOSITES)") instead, where children are registered in the parent and
    the root manifest stays a single record.

    bench/scaling.pl records child-creation scaling, working-set cache
    costs, process RSS, and wall-time latency percentiles for concurrent
    increments. Its samples are workload measurements, not latency
    guarantees. Child counts and file-cache LRU updates now take constant
    time during normal operation. Bound compiled dispatch is owned by the
    root, so retaining a compiled path does not retain an unmounted native
    handle. Backend minimum versions are the validated baselines recorded in
    Data::Shared::Registry; they are published as the recommended minima and
    enforced at first backend use. Nothing checks them at installation,
    because the leaves are not required.

CLASSIFICATION OF THE 39 LEAVES
    Map-like: HashMap (10 variants), Buffer (11), RadixTree, Intern, Pool
    (+I64/I32/F64/Str), PerfectHash (read-only). Set-like: BloomFilter,
    CountingBloomFilter, CuckooFilter, BitSet, RoaringBitmap, SortedSet
    (+Strings), DisjointSet. Queue-like: Queue, Deque, Stack, Heap,
    RingBuffer, Log, PubSub. Indexed: NDArray, Fenwick, Fenwick2D,
    SegmentTree, KDTree, IntervalTree, SpatialHash, Graph. Sketch/stats:
    HyperLogLog, CountMinSketch, MinHash, TopK, DDSketch, Histogram,
    Reservoir. Sync/timing (call-only): Sync (Semaphore, Barrier, RWLock,
    Condvar, Once), TimingWheel, HierTimingWheel, ReqRep.

SECURITY
    A file-backed root is created mode 0700, and the manifest and the
    ".manifest.lock" that serialises topology changes are created 0600.
    Republishing the manifest keeps whatever mode it already had, so a root
    you widened on purpose stays widened. Backing files come from the
    leaves, which create them 0600 with "O_NOFOLLOW" and "O_EXCL". A
    directory or lock that already exists keeps its own mode, so sharing a
    root between users stays an explicit choice; the facade does not offer a
    mode argument for it, and a group-writable root lets any member of that
    group unlink the manifest.

    The manifest is trusted input. Whatever can write it chooses which
    mounts this process opens and which backing files it maps, so a writer
    who also controls those files controls what the process attaches to: a
    recorded argument list is not re-applied to a file that already exists,
    and the leaf rejects a file whose header does not match the declared
    type, but the file itself decides the mapping. An "args" entry naming an
    absolute path is honoured, so a manifest can reach a file outside the
    root.

    Put the root where only its trusted writers can create it. Because an
    existing directory keeps its own mode and the manifest inside it is
    trusted, a root named under a world-writable directory -- /tmp on a
    shared host, as the examples here spell it for brevity -- can be
    pre-created by anyone, who then chooses what the first real process
    attaches to.

    Mount names are sanitised into the root directory, so a name containing
    ".." or "/" cannot place a backing file outside it. Anything you grant
    write access to a shared mapping is trusted not to corrupt it while
    others are attached, as with the leaves.

SEE ALSO
    Data::Path::XS (the API model), Data::Shared::Registry (the inventory
    and verb tables), Data::Shared::Cookbook (per-dist recipes), and each
    "Data::*::Shared" leaf for its native semantics.

